    slug: 'cuet-math-normalization-2026-percentiles',
    title: "CUET Math Normalization 2026 Explained: Deep Dive into Percentile System",
    description: "CUET math normalization 2026 explained — how NTA calculates percentiles, equipercentile method, and what students need to know about score normalization.",
    date: "13 अगस्त 2026, दोपहर 12 बजकर 45 मिनट",
    modifiedDate: "August 13, 2026",
    author: "Amit Kumar",
    authorName: "Amit Kumar",
    authorSlug: "amit-kumar",
    authorRole: "Entrance Exam Specialist",
    authorBio: "With a 90% success rate, Amit Kumar breaks down complex Math and Reasoning concepts for JNVST and Sainik School aspirants. He focuses on speed and accuracy.",
    readTime: '3 mins',
    category: "Admissions & Test Prep",
    image: "/images/blog/cuet-math-normalization-2026-percentiles.webp"
    ,
    content: `<p>CUET UG 2026 results use a normalization process to ensure fairness across different exam shifts. Understanding how NTA calculates percentiles and normalized scores is crucial for students planning their university admissions.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is Score Normalization?</h2>
<p>CUET is conducted in multiple shifts over several days. Each shift has a different question paper with slightly different difficulty levels. Normalization ensures that a student's score is not unfairly affected by the difficulty level of their specific shift.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How NTA Normalization Works</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1 — Raw Score:</strong> Your actual marks in the exam (e.g., 180 out of 250).</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 — Percentile Calculation:</strong> Your percentile shows how you performed relative to other students in the same shift. Formula: Percentile = (Number of students with score less than yours / Total students) × 100.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 — Equipercentile Method:</strong> NTA uses this method to compare percentiles across different shifts. If two students have the same percentile in different shifts, they get the same normalized score.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CUET Marks vs Percentile (Expected)</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="min-w-full bg-white border border-gray-200 shadow-sm rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm"><thead class="bg-[#035c00]"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Total NTA Score (5 Subjects)</th><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Expected Percentile</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">1150+</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-green-700">99+ percentile</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">1000-1150</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold">95-99 percentile</td></tr><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">850-1000</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold">85-95 percentile</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">700-850</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">70-85 percentile</td></tr><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 font-medium text-gray-900">Below 700</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Below 70 percentile</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Common Misconceptions</h2>
<p><strong>Misconception 1:</strong> "My raw marks are low, so my percentile will be low." — Not true. If your shift was difficult, a lower raw score can still give a high percentile.</p>
<p><strong>Misconception 2:</strong> "Normalization reduces my score." — Normalization can increase or decrease your score depending on your shift's difficulty. It ensures fairness.</p>
<p><strong>Misconception 3:</strong> "I can compare my raw score with students from other shifts." — You cannot. Only normalized/percentile scores are comparable across shifts.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Universities See</h2>
<p>Universities receive your <strong>NTA score (normalized)</strong>, not your raw marks. They use this score for admission decisions. Some universities also consider your Class 12 board marks along with CUET scores.</p>

<div class="bg-[#035c00]/5 border border-[#035c00]/20 rounded-xl p-5 my-6"><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">CUET Guidance</h3><p>JGPS School Joya provides guidance for CUET preparation and university admissions. Call <strong>+91 9412137554</strong> for details.</p></div>

<div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6">
<h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">August 2026 Update</h3>
<p>CUET UG 2026 results with normalized scores are available on <strong>cuet.nta.nic.in</strong>. Counselling process has started. Students should check their normalized NTA scores for university admissions. Source: NTA (August 2026).</p>
</div>`,
