    faqs:     [{'question': 'What is the most effective study habit?', 'answer': 'Active recall — testing yourself instead of re-reading — is the most effective study technique according to research from Harvard and Stanford.'}, {'question': 'How does spaced repetition work?', 'answer': 'Review material at increasing intervals: Day 1, Day 3, Day 7, Day 14. This moves information from short-term to long-term memory.'}, {'question': 'What is the Pomodoro technique?', 'answer': 'Study for 25 minutes, take a 5-minute break. Repeat 4 times, then take a 30-minute break. This prevents mental fatigue.'}, {'question': 'How many hours should I study daily?', 'answer': 'Consistency matters more than hours. 2-3 focused hours daily is better than 10 hours on weekends. Use active recall and spaced repetition.'}, {'question': 'What is the Feynman Technique?', 'answer': "Named after Richard Feynman, it means explaining a topic in simple language as if teaching a child. If you can't explain it simply, you don't understand it well enough."}],
